  "worked_solution": "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+1)}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2{x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\,).\n$$",
